WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.760
Hi, Yosha. I'm really glad you could join us here today.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
I'm very glad to.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.960
You know, today you're not just a researcher or engineer in deep learning,

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.960
you've become one of the institutions or one of the icons of deep learning.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.560
But I'd really like to hear the story of how it started.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.960
So how did you end up, you know, getting into deep learning and then pursuing this journey?

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.960
Right. Well, actually, it started when I was a kid, adolescent, reading a lot of science fiction.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.960
Like, I guess, many of us.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:43.960
And when I started my graduate studies in 1985, I started reading, you know, lab papers.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.960
And that's where I got all excited and it became really a passion.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.960
And actually, what was that like in what, mid 80s, right? 1985, reading these papers.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:53.960
Do you remember?

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.960
Yeah, it was.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:20.960
Well, you know, coming from the courses I had taken in classical A.I. with expert systems and suddenly discovering that there was all this world of thinking about how humans might be learning and human intelligence and how we might draw connections between that and artificial intelligence and computers.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.960
That was really exciting for me when I discovered this literature and I started reading the connectionists, of course.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.960
So the papers from Jeff Hinton, Rummel Hart and so on.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.960
And I worked on recurrent nets.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.960
I worked on speech recognition.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.960
I worked on HMM, so graphical models.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:49.960
And then quickly moved to AT&T, Bell Labs and MIT, where I did postdocs.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:56.960
And that's where I discovered some of the issues with the long term dependencies with training neural nets.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:06.960
And then shortly after I got recruited at UDM back in Montreal, where I had spent most of my child's adolescence years.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:15.960
You know, and so as someone who's been there for the last several decades and seen, you know, seen it all, certainly seen a lot of it.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.960
Tell me about how you're thinking about deep learning about neural networks as evolved over this time.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.960
We started with experiments, with intuitions and theory sort of comes later.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:34.960
We now understand a lot better, for example, why backdrop is working so well, why depth is so important.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:41.960
And, you know, these kinds of notions, we didn't have any solid justification for in those days.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:49.960
When we started working on deep nets in the early 2000s, we had the intuition that it made a lot of sense that a deeper network should be more powerful.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.960
And we didn't know how to take that and prove it.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.960
And of course, our experiments initially didn't work.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.960
And actually, what are the most important things that you think turned out to be right?

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.960
And what were the biggest surprises of what turned out to be wrong, you know, compared to what we knew 30 years ago?

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:24.960
Sure. So one of the biggest mistakes I made was to think that, like everyone else in the 90s, that you needed smooth nonlinearities in order for backdrop to work.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:37.960
Because I thought that, you know, if we had something like the rectifying nonlinearities where you have a flat part, that it would be really hard to train because the derivative would be zero in so many places.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:54.960
And when we started experimenting with relu with deep nets in around 2010, you know, I was obsessed with the idea that, oh, we should be careful about whether neurons won't saturate too much on the zero part.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:02.960
But in the end, it turned out that actually the relu was working a lot better than the sigmoids and the tach. And that was a big surprise.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:12.960
We did this exploring this because of the biological connection, actually, not because we thought that it would be easier to optimize, but it turned out work better.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.960
Whereas I thought it would be harder to train.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:23.960
So let me ask you, what is the relationship between deep learning and the brain? There's the obvious answer, but I'm curious, what's your answer to that?

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:47.960
Well, the initial insight that really got me excited with neural nets was this idea from the connectionists that information is distributed across the activation of many neurons rather than being represented by sort of the grandmother cell, as they were calling it,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:57.960
a symbolic representation that was the traditional view in classical AI. And I still believe this is a really important thing.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:12.960
And I see people rediscovering the importance of that even recently. So that was really a foundation. The death thing is something that came later in the early 2000s.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.960
But it wasn't something I was thinking about in the 90s, for example.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:26.960
Right. I remember you build a lot of relatively shallow but very distributed representations or the word embeddings very early on.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:39.960
That's right. That's right. Yeah, that's that's one of the things that I got really excited about in the late 90s with actually my brother, Sunny, and I worked on the idea that we could use neural nets to tackle the curse of dimensionality,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:56.960
which was believed to be one of the central issues with statistical learning. And the fact that we could have these distributed representations could be used to represent joint distributions over many random variables in a very efficient way.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:14.960
And it turned out to work quite well. And then I extended this to join institutions over sequences of words. And this is how the word embeddings were born, because I thought, oh, you know, this will allow generalization across words that have similar semantic meaning and so on.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:28.960
So over the last couple of decades, your research group has invented more ideas than anyone can summarize in a few minutes. So I'm curious, what are the inventions or ideas you're most proud of from your group?

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:39.960
Right. So I think I mentioned long term dependencies, the study of that, I think people still don't understand it well enough.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:47.920
Then there's the story I mentioned about curse of dimensionality, joint distributions with neural nets, which became more recently the nade that Hugo Don

00:06:47.920 --> 00:07:07.960
Rochelle did. And then, as I said that gave rise to also the work on learning word embeddings for joint distributions for words. Then came, I think, probably the best known events of the work we did with deep learning with stacks of auto encoders and stacks of RBMs.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:23.960
One thing then, it was the work on understanding better the difficulties of training deep nets with Xavier Laurel with the initialization ideas and also the vanishing gradient in deep nets.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:34.960
And that work actually was the one which gave rise to the experiments showing the importance of piecewise linear activation functions.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:47.960
Then I would say some of the most important work regards the work we did with unsupervised learning, the denoising on encoders, the GANs, which are very popular these days, the narrative adversarial networks.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:08:00.960
The work we did with neural machine translation using attention, which turned out to be really important for making attention, making translation work.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:08.960
And it's currently used in industrial systems like Google Translate. But this attention thing actually really changed my views on neural nets.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:18.960
You know, neural nets, we used to think as machines that can map a vector to a vector. But really with attention mechanisms, you can now handle any kind of data structure.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.960
And this is really opening up a lot of interesting avenues.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:36.960
The direction of actually connecting to biology, one thing that I've been working on in the last couple of years is how could we come up with something like backprop that brains could implement?

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:46.960
And we have a few papers in that direction that seems to be interesting for the neuroscience people. And then we're continuing on that direction, of course.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:55.960
One of the topics that I know you've been thinking a lot about is the relationship between deep learning and the brain. Can you tell us a bit more about that?

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:07.960
The biological thing is something I've been thinking about for a while, actually, and having a lot of, I would say, daydreaming about.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:20.960
Because it's like, I think of it like a puzzle. So we have these pieces of evidence from what we know from the brain and from learning in the brain, like spike timing, dependent plasticity.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:34.960
And on the other hand, we have all of these concepts from machine learning, the idea of globally training the whole system with respect to an objective function and the idea of backprop.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:40.960
And what does backprop mean? What does credit assignment really mean?

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:57.960
When I started thinking about how brains could do something like backprop, it prompted me to think about, well, maybe there's some more general concepts behind backprop which make it so efficient, which allows us to be efficient with backprop.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:06.960
Maybe there's a larger family of ways to do credit assignment, and that connects to questions that people in reinforcement learning have been asking.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:23.960
So it's interesting how sometimes asking a simple question leads you to thinking about so many different things and forces you to think about so many elements that you'd like to bring together like a big puzzle.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:34.960
So this has gone for a number of years. I need to say that this whole endeavor, like many of the ones that I have followed, has been highly inspired by Jeff Hinton's thoughts.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:11:00.960
So in particular, he gave this talk in 2007, I think, the first deep learning workshop on what he thought was the way that the brain is working, how temporal code could be used for potentially doing some of the job of backprop.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:15.960
And that led to a lot of the ideas that I've explored in recent years with this. Yeah, so it's kind of an interesting story that has been running for a decade now, basically.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:24.960
One of the topics I've heard you speak about multiple times as well is unsupervised learning. Can you share your perspective on that?

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:41.960
Yes, yes. So unsupervised learning is really important. Right now, our industrial systems are based on supervised learning, which essentially requires humans to define what the important concepts are for the problem and to label those concepts in the data.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:59.960
And we build all these amazing toys and services and systems using this. But humans are able to do much more. They're able to explore and discover new concepts by observation and interaction with the world.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:13.960
A two-year-old is able to understand intuitive physics. In other words, she understands gravity, she understands pressure, she understands inertia, she understands liquid solids.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:20.960
And of course, her parents never told her about any of these stuff. Right? So how did she figure it out?

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:29.960
So that's the kind of question that unsupervised learning is trying to answer. It's not just about we have labels or we don't have labels.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:44.960
It's about actually building a construction, a mental construction that explains how the world works by observation. And more recently, I've been combining the ideas in unsupervised learning with the ideas of reinforcement learning.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:13:07.960
Because I believe that there is a very strong indication about the important underlying concepts that we're trying to disentangle when trying to separate from each other that a human or machine can get by interacting with the world, by exploring the world and trying things and trying to control things.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:09.960
So these are, I think, tightly coupled to the original ideas of unsupervised learning. So my take on unsupervised learning 15 years ago when we started doing the auto-enquoted

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:33.960
and the RBM's and so on was very focused on the idea of learning good representations. And I still think this is a central question. But the thing we don't know is what is a good representation?

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:49.960
How do we figure out an objective function, for example? So we've tried many things over the years. And that's actually one of the cool things about unsupervised learning research, that there are so many different ideas, so different ways that this problem can be attacked.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:58.960
And that suggests maybe there's another one we'll discover next year that's completely different and maybe the brain is using something else completely different.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:13.960
So it's not incremental research. It's something that in itself is very exploratory. We don't have a good definition of what's the right objective function to even measure that a system is doing a good job of unsupervised learning.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:27.960
So of course it's challenging, but at the same time it leaves open a wide field of possibilities, which is what researchers really love. At least that's something that appeals to me.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:37.960
So today there's so much going on in deep learning. And I think we've passed the point where it's possible for anyone human to read every single deep learning paper being published.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:43.960
So I'm curious, what in deep learning today excites you to know?

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:53.960
So I'm very ambitious. And I feel like the current state of science of deep learning is far from where I'd like to see it.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:15:05.960
And I have the impression that our systems right now make mistakes that suggest, the kind of mistakes that suggest that they have a very superficial understanding of the world.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:14.960
So what excites me the most now is sort of direction of research where we're not trying to build systems and we're going to do something useful.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:25.960
We're just going back to principles about how can a computer observe the world, interact with the world, and discover how that world works.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:32.960
Even if that world is simple, something that we can program is a kind of video game, we don't know how to do that well.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:37.960
And that's cool because I don't have to compete with Google and Facebook and Baidu and so on, right?

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:44.960
Because this is a kind of basic research that can be done by anyone in their garage and could change the world.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:56.960
So there are many, of course, many directions to attack this, but I see a lot of the fruitful interactions between ideas and deep learning and reinforcement learning being really important there.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:15.960
And I'm really excited that the progress in this direction could have a huge impact on practical applications, actually, because if you look at some of the big challenges that we have in applications like how we deal with new domains or categories on which we have too few examples.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:23.960
And in cases where humans are very good at solving those problems, so these transfer learning and generalization issues,

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:33.960
they would become much easier to tackle if we have systems that had a better understanding of how the world works, a deeper understanding.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:42.960
What is actually going on? What are the causes of what I'm seeing and how could I influence what I'm seeing by my actions?

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:48.960
So these are the kinds of questions I'm really excited about these days.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:17:06.960
And I think they connect also the deep learning research that has evolved over the last couple of decades with even older questions in AI, because a lot of the success in deep learning has been with perception.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:13.960
So what's left, right? What's left is sort of higher level cognition, which is about understanding at an abstract level how things work.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:21.960
So our program of understanding high level abstractions, I think, has not reached those high level of abstractions.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:27.960
And so we have to get there. We have to think about reasoning, about sequential processing of information.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:37.960
We have to think of how causality works and how machines can discover all these things by themselves, potentially guided by humans, but as much as possible in an autonomous way.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:50.960
And it sounds like from part of what you said that you're a fan of research approaches where you experiment on, you know, I'm going to use the term toy problem, not in a disparaging way, but on a small problem.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.960
And you're optimistic that that transfers to bigger problems later.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:18:06.960
Yes, yes, yes. I mean, it transfers in a in a meta way, right? Of course, we're going to have to do some work to scale up and address those problems.

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:25.960
But my main motivation for going for those toy problems is that we can understand better our failures and we can reduce the problem to something we can intuitively sort of manipulate and understand more easily.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:37.960
So sort of a classical divide and conquer, you know, science approach. And also, I think something people don't think about enough is the cycle, the research cycle can be much faster, right?

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:54.960
So if I can do an experiment in a few hours, I can progress much faster. If I have to train a huge model that tries to capture the whole common sense and, you know, everything in the general knowledge, which will eventually will do.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:19:05.960
It's just each experiment just takes too much time with current hardware. So while our hardware friends are building machines that are going to be a thousand or a million times faster, I'm doing those toy experiments.

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:18.980
You know, I've also heard you speak about the science of deep learning, not just as an engineering discipline, but doing more work to understand what's really going on.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:21.980
Do you share your thoughts on that?

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:30.980
Yeah, absolutely. I fear that a lot of the work that we're doing is sort of like blind people trying to find their way.

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:48.980
And, you know, you can get a lot of luck and find interesting things that way. But really, if we sort of stop a little bit and try to understand what we're doing in a way that's transferable because we go down to principles to theory.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:20:00.980
But when I say theory, I don't mean necessarily math. Right. I don't I'm not like, of course, I like math and so on. But but I don't think that we need that everything be formalized mathematically, but be formalized logically.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:08.980
Now, the sense that I can convince somebody that, you know, this should work with that. This makes sense. This is the most important aspect.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:29.980
And then math allows us to make that stronger and tighter. But really, it's more about understanding. And it's about also doing our research not to beat the next baseline or a benchmark or beat the other guys in the other lab or the other company.

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:42.980
It's more about, you know, what kind of question should we ask that would allow us to understand better the phenomenon of interest? Like, you know, what makes, for example, training in deeper networks harder?

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:48.980
We can't net harder. We have some ideas, but a lot of things we don't understand yet.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:21:04.980
So we can maybe design experiments whose goal is not to have like a better algorithm, but just to understand better the algorithms we currently have or why what circumstances make particular algorithms work better and why I mean, it's the why that really matters.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.980
That's what science is about. It's why.

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:20.980
Today, there are a lot of people that want to enter the fields. And I'll show you, Ansa does a lot in one on one settings. But, you know, with all the people watching this on video, what advice would you have for people that want to get into AI again as a deep learning?

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:35.980
Right. So first of all, there are different motivations, different things you could do. You know, what you need to become a deep learning researcher may not be the same as if you want to be an engineer who's going to use deep learning to build products.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:44.980
There's a different level of understanding that's needed in both cases. But in any case, in both cases, practice.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:56.980
So to really master a subject like deep learning, of course, you have to read a lot. You have to practice programming the things yourself.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:13.980
Very often, I, you know, I interview students who have used software. And these days, there's so so many, you know, so so good software around that you can just, you know, plug and play and understand nothing of what you're doing, or at such a superficial level that then it becomes hard to figure out when it doesn't work.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:22.980
What's going wrong? So actually trying to implement things yourself, even if it's inefficient, but just to make sure you really understand what is going on is really useful.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:25.980
And, you know, trying to implement yourself.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:32.980
So don't just use one of the programming frameworks, you can do everything in a few lines of code, but you don't really know what just happened, right?

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:51.980
Exactly, exactly. And I would say even more than that, try to derive the thing yourself from, you know, first principles, if you can. So that really helps. But, yeah, I mean, the usual things you have to do, like reading, looking at other people's code, writing your own code,

00:22:51.980 --> 00:23:03.980
doing a lot of experiment, making sure you understand everything you do. So I mean, especially for the science part of it, trying to ask, why am I doing this? Why are people doing this?

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:12.980
And maybe the answer is somewhere in the book, and you have to read more. But it's even better if you can actually figure it out by yourself.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:22.980
Cool. Yeah. And in fact, of the things that read, you and Ian Goofalo and Aaron Koval wrote a very highly regarded book.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:41.980
Thank you. Thank you. Yes, it's selling a lot. It's a bit crazy. I feel like there's more people reading this book than people who can read it right now. But yeah, also proceedings of the ICLR ICLR conference is probably the best way to do it.

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:58.980
It's a really nice, concentrated place of good papers. Of course, there are really good papers at NIPS and ICML and other conferences. But if you really want to go for a lot of good papers, just read the last few ICLR proceedings, and that will give you a really good view of the field.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:07.980
Cool. Yeah. Any other thoughts when people ask you for advice? You know, how does someone, right, become good at deep learning?

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:25.980
Well, it depends on where you come from. Don't be afraid by the math. Just, you know, just develop the intuitions, and then the math becomes really easier to understand once you get the hang of what's going on at the intuitive level.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:47.980
And one good news is that you don't need five years of PhD to become proficient at deep learning. You can actually learn pretty quickly. If you have a good background in computer science and math, you can learn enough to use it and build things and start research experiments in just a few months.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:58.980
Something like six months for people with the right training, maybe they don't know anything about machine learning, but if they're good in math and computer science, it can be very fast.

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:11.980
And of course, so that means you need to have the right training in math and computer science. Sometimes what you learn in just computer science courses is not enough. You need some continuous math, especially.

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:18.980
So this is probability algebra and optimization, for example.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:20.980
And calculus.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:22.980
And calculus. Yeah.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:33.980
Thanks a lot, Joshua, for sharing all the comments and insights and advice. Even though I've known you for a long time, there are many details of your early history that I didn't know until now. So thank you.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:46.980
Well, thank you, Andrew, for doing this special recording and what you're doing. And well, I hope it's going to be used by a lot of people.
